1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,023
Gate needs billing. At 06:00 there are
three billing pods. At 06:12 one dies
during a deploy. At 06:15 a fourth starts
on a different node.

2
00:00:12,143 --> 00:00:28,463
If gate still dials http://10.0.4.22:8080,
check-ins fail while healthy instances sit
idle. Discovery answers a simple question:
given the logical name billing-service,
which live instances exist right now?

3
00:00:28,643 --> 00:00:42,131
A registry holds the catalog. Each
instance registers on startup with host,
port, health URL, and metadata. Clients —
or a platform DNS layer — query the
catalog and choose a target.

4
00:00:42,251 --> 00:00:54,035
In classic Spring Cloud demos that
registry is Eureka. On Kubernetes, CoreDNS
and Endpoints often play the same role;
Spring Cloud Kubernetes can adapt the
programming model.

5
00:00:54,155 --> 00:01:08,867
The pattern matters more than the brand:
register, heartbeat, fetch, call by name.
Look at this example. # billing-service
Look at this example. # gate-service Look
at this java for a moment.

6
00:01:08,987 --> 00:01:21,587
At runtime, billing heartbeats. Eureka (or
your platform) marks it UP. Gate’s
DiscoveryClient.getInstances("billing-
service") returns the three healthy URIs.

7
00:01:21,707 --> 00:01:31,187
Higher-level clients — load-balanced
WebClient, Feign, Gateway lb:// URIs —
consume that list so application code
never embeds pod IPs.

8
00:01:31,307 --> 00:01:38,003
Self-preservation and lease timeouts
matter: a flapping network should not
empty the registry and black-hole the
quay,

9
00:01:38,123 --> 00:01:44,675
but a dead pod that stops renewing must
leave the catalog within tens of seconds
or gate keeps selecting a corpse.

10
00:01:44,795 --> 00:01:57,299
Walk a deploy failure. Billing pod B2
stops heartbeats at 06:12. Until the lease
expires, gate may still receive B2 in the
instance list and open sockets that time
out.

11
00:01:57,419 --> 00:02:10,451
Symptom at the booth: intermittent 504s on
check-in while two other pods are fine.
Your debug probe still shows three rows
until expiry; after expiry it shows two,
then three again when B4 registers.

12
00:02:10,571 --> 00:02:22,283
That timeline is why registry-fetch-
interval-seconds and lease settings are
operational knobs, not decoration. Too
aggressive and you thrash; too slow and
you serve dead IPs through a whole truck
queue.

13
00:02:22,403 --> 00:02:33,731
Health integration is not optional
decoration. If billing’s /actuator/health
goes DOWN because Postgres is unreachable,
the registry should stop handing that
instance to gate.

14
00:02:33,851 --> 00:02:46,259
Otherwise discovery lies and load
balancers keep selecting a dying pod.
Prefer-IP versus hostname, zone metadata,
and secure-port flags show up the first
time TLS or multi-AZ routing is real.

15
00:02:46,379 --> 00:02:52,715
Zone quay-a on the instance is how later
load balancers prefer local billing when
cross-quay latency hurts.

16
00:02:52,835 --> 00:02:59,291
Trade-offs: a Java registry (Eureka) gives
Spring-native metadata and demos that
teach the model;

17
00:02:59,411 --> 00:03:06,947
Kubernetes DNS gives fewer moving parts if
every service already lives in the cluster
and you accept platform-shaped discovery.

18
00:03:07,067 --> 00:03:19,571
Mixing both without a clear owner — some
Feign clients on DNS, some on Eureka —
produces “works in staging, wrong host in
prod” nights. Pick one source of truth for
billing-service and make gate’s clients
use it.

19
00:03:19,691 --> 00:03:29,171
A misconception is hard-coding hostnames
“temporarily” beside discovery and then
shipping both paths — callers diverge and
on-call cannot tell which URL is
authoritative.

20
00:03:29,291 --> 00:03:36,611
Another is assuming discovery replaces
load balancing; discovery gives you
candidates, balancing chooses among them.

21
00:03:36,731 --> 00:03:47,195
A third is registering every batch job and
one-off tool into the same registry
namespace until billing-service returns
noise. Gate can now ask for billing-
service by name.

22
00:03:47,315 --> 00:03:59,459
Truckers on the public internet still
should not open a VPN to every internal
pod. Something at the edge must accept one
hostname and route inward. That edge is
the API gateway.
